-- title:
--   Finiteness of n-torsion of Pic⁰ in characteristic zero
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ and $F$ be fields with $F$ a $K$-algebra, $K$ algebraically closed of characteristic zero. Assume $F$ contains an element $x$ transcendental over $K$ such that $F$ is finite-dimensional over the intermediate field $K(x)$ generated by $x$, and assume `IsCurveOver K F`: every nonzero $f \in F$ admits a divisor $D$ with $D(v) = \mathrm{ord}_v(f)$ at every place $v$ and $\deg D = 0$, every place of $F/K$ has residue field finite over $K$, and the module of Kähler differentials $\Omega[F/K]$ is free of rank one over $F$; here a place is a valuation subring of $F$ containing the image of $K$, different from $F$ itself, and a principal ideal ring. Let $n$ be a natural number with $n \neq 0$. Then the subgroup of $n$-torsion elements of $\mathrm{Pic}^0(F/K)$ is finite, where $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ is the group of finitely supported $\mathbb{Z}$-valued functions on places of degree zero modulo those of the form $v \mapsto \mathrm{ord}_v(f)$ for some nonzero $f \in F$; finiteness is asserted for the subtype of $c$ with $n \cdot c = 0$.
--
--   This is the finiteness half of Weil's count $\#\mathrm{Pic}^0[n] = n^{2g}$ for a function field in one variable over an algebraically closed field, here in characteristic zero only. It is used in the descent of torsion divisor classes along extensions of algebraically closed constant fields, and downstream in the construction of the torsion data attached to modular curves.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicCurve_Pic0_finite_torsion_of_isAlgClosed_of_charZero.lean

import Mathlib
